OpenBDS - third-party and proprietary material
==============================================

This repository contains two very different categories of content. Only the
first one is open source.

1. OpenBDS engine and project files (MIT licensed)
--------------------------------------------------

Everything under `bin/`, `src/`, `tools/`, `test/`, `scripts/`, `deploy/` and
`docs/`, plus project metadata (package.json, Dockerfile, workflows, ...), is
licensed under the MIT License - see LICENSE. Contributions are welcome under
the same terms (see CONTRIBUTING.md).

The OpenBDS engine is an independent, clean-room implementation of a headless
Minecraft: Bedrock server. It was written against publicly documented protocol
behavior and open-source protocol definitions; it is not derived from Mojang
server source code or from a decompilation of the proprietary server binary.

2. Mojang / Microsoft proprietary material (NOT open source)
------------------------------------------------------------

The following files are the property of Mojang Studios / Microsoft and are
redistributed here (or referenced via Git LFS) only as part of a working
Minecraft: Bedrock Edition dedicated-server distribution. They are covered by
the Minecraft EULA and the Minecraft Dedicated Server EULA, NOT by the MIT
license above:

  * bedrock_server                (server binary, stored with Git LFS)
  * bedrock_server_how_to.html
  * release-notes.txt
  * behavior_packs/               (vanilla and chemistry data/behavior packs)
  * resource_packs/               (vanilla resource packs, __brarchive data)
  * definitions/                  (attachables, features, spawn groups, ...)
  * data/                         (bootstrap data)
  * config/default/               (default configuration data)
  * server.properties             (Mojang-shipped default configuration)
  * packetlimitconfig.json
  * profanity_filter.wlist
  * allowlist.json / permissions.json defaults as shipped by Mojang

You may use these files to run a Minecraft: Bedrock Edition server subject to
Mojang's terms (https://www.minecraft.net/eula). You may NOT relicense them as
open source. If you publish a fork or mirror, keep this NOTICE intact and keep
the Mojang material clearly marked as proprietary. If you want a fully
open-source tree, delete the files listed above and run the OpenBDS engine on
its own - it does not require them.

